Chapter: DiagSink
  cites Foreword chapter TextSearch
  cites Works chapter GopFat16
  cites Works chapter GopDisk
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc
  cites Diag chapter DiagStage

 The sink ladder from apps/works/SinkLadderProbe.codex as a stage: the
 2.7 MB streamed write (WORKS-9's question) through the same FAT16 writer
 the bank uses, on the medium the bank opened, then read back and compared
 byte for byte. `sink shift=1` in DIAG.CFG is the oracle calibration: every
 byte is then reported bad and the row must say so (L-FALSIF).

 We say:

Section: States

  dsk-states : List Text = ["ok", "recovered", "no-medium", "mount-fail", "write-refused", "died", "size-bad", "read-fail", "bad-bytes", "ladder-all", "ladder-stop", "rung-1-refused"]

  dsk-size : Integer = 2745998
  dsk-chunk : Integer = 65536

Section: Payload

  dsk-pat : Integer -> Integer
  dsk-pat (i) = int-mod (i * 7 + i / 513) 251

  dsk-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dsk-fill (buf) (i) (stop) =
    if i >= stop then 0
    else let w = poke-byte buf i (dsk-pat i)
    in dsk-fill buf (i + 1) stop

  dsk-fill-all : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dsk-fill-all (buf) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let stop = if i + dsk-chunk > n then n else i + dsk-chunk
    in let f = dsk-fill buf i stop
    in dsk-fill-all buf stop n

  dsk-bad : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dsk-bad (buf) (i) (n) (shift) (bad) =
    if i >= n then bad
    else if peek-byte buf i /= dsk-pat (i + shift) then dsk-bad buf (i + 1) n shift (bad + 1)
    else dsk-bad buf (i + 1) n shift bad

  dsk-name-buf : Integer -> Integer
  dsk-name-buf (dummy) =
    let nb = alloc-zeroed 16 16
    in let a = poke-byte nb 0 83
    in let b = poke-byte nb 1 73
    in let c = poke-byte nb 2 78
    in let d = poke-byte nb 3 75
    in let e = poke-byte nb 4 32
    in let f = poke-byte nb 5 32
    in let g = poke-byte nb 6 32
    in let h = poke-byte nb 7 32
    in let i = poke-byte nb 8 67
    in let j = poke-byte nb 9 68
    in let k = poke-byte nb 10 88
    in nb

Section: The Stage

 `sink chunk=N` sets the MSC chunk for this stage, cell 91, zero for the
 default of 64 sectors. Sitting 4 banked wr=0 twice, so it is the FIRST
 chunk of the data run that refuses; the LBA moves only because the file's
 allocation moves. A flight at a smaller chunk is what separates "the
 transfer is too big" from "the endpoint halts whatever we send", and the
 bed cannot answer it because the bed completes the transfer at any size.

  dsk-msc-chunk : DiagCtx -> Integer
  dsk-msc-chunk (c) =
    let v = diag-cfg-value c "sink"
    in if text-starts-with v "chunk=" then text-to-integer (substring v 6 (text-length v - 6)) else 0

  dsk-shift : DiagCtx -> Integer
  dsk-shift (c) =
    let v = diag-cfg-value c "sink"
    in if text-starts-with v "shift=" then text-to-integer (substring v 6 (text-length v - 6)) else 0

 The medium's own answer to the refusing transfer is already banked by the
 MSC layer and by the writer; nothing read it out, so the 2026-08-19 metal
 row said only WHERE the write stopped. `after` is -1 when no small write
 was attempted.

 The snapshot is taken BEFORE the small write, because that write is itself
 a transfer and overwrites every cell here.

  DskSnap = record {
    ds-wr : Integer,
    ds-cc : Integer,
    ds-lba : Integer,
    ds-rty : Integer,
    ds-ph : Integer
  }

  dsk-snap : Integer -> DskSnap
  dsk-snap (dummy) =
    DskSnap { ds-wr = xdiag-get gfat-cell-written, ds-cc = xdiag-get msc-cell-lastcc, ds-lba = xdiag-get msc-cell-fail-lba, ds-rty = xdiag-get msc-cell-retry, ds-ph = xdiag-get msc-cell-phase }

  dsk-lines : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, DskSnap, Integer -> List Text
  dsk-lines (size) (got) (bad) (shift) (wstage) (s) (after) =
    ["size=" & show size & " read=" & show got & " bad=" & show bad & " shift=" & show shift & " wstage=" & show wstage,
     "wr=" & show (s.ds-wr) & " cc=" & show (s.ds-cc) & " lba=" & show (s.ds-lba) & " rty=" & show (s.ds-rty) & " ph=" & show (s.ds-ph) & " after=" & show after & " chunk=" & show (msc-chunk-now 0)]

 A refused write and a dead target are different events on the wire and were
 one state until now. A refusal COMPLETES: the transfer event arrives and
 carries a status. A target that has stopped answering posts no transfer
 event at all, which the driver reports as `msc-ce-no-event` (256) after its
 poll fuel runs out, and `msc-cell-fail-lba` names the write it died on.

  dsk-write-state : DskSnap -> Text
  dsk-write-state (s) =
    if (s.ds-cc) == msc-ce-no-event then "died" else "write-refused"

 A write that SUCCEEDED after the driver reset the endpoint and retried is
 not the same event as one that never faltered, and the glass said `ok` for
 both. `msc-cell-retry` reads `msc-retry-ok` when a chunk failed and the
 retry after recovery carried it, so that is the discriminator.

 The cell is cleared before the stage's own write and not merely read after
 it. Nothing else scopes it: the MSC layer sets it once at init and then only
 on a retry, so a recovery in ANY earlier stage would still be sitting in it
 and this stage would report a recovery it never performed.

  dsk-ok-state : Integer -> Text
  dsk-ok-state (dummy) =
    if xdiag-get msc-cell-retry == msc-retry-ok then "recovered" else "ok"

  dsk-finish : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> DiagResult
  dsk-finish (state) (colour) (size) (got) (bad) (shift) (wstage) =
    dsk-finish-in state colour size got bad shift wstage (dsk-snap 0) (-1)

  dsk-finish-in : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, DskSnap, Integer -> DiagResult
  dsk-finish-in (state) (colour) (size) (got) (bad) (shift) (wstage) (s) (after) =
    diag-result state colour (dsk-lines size got bad shift wstage s after) (dsk-lines size got bad shift wstage s after)

  dsk-verify : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Port] DiagResult
  dsk-verify (vol) (shift) = act
    sz <- gfat-file-size vol "SINK.CDX"
    if sz /= dsk-size then dsk-finish "size-bad" diag-col-bad sz 0 0 shift (xdiag-get gfat-cell-write)
    else act
      f <- gfat-read-file vol "SINK.CDX"
      if f.gf-ok == False then dsk-finish "read-fail" diag-col-bad sz (f.gf-len) 0 shift (xdiag-get gfat-cell-write)
      else let bad = dsk-bad (f.gf-buf) 0 (f.gf-len) shift 0
      in if bad /= 0 then dsk-finish "bad-bytes" diag-col-bad sz (f.gf-len) bad shift (xdiag-get gfat-cell-write)
      else dsk-finish (dsk-ok-state 0) (if (dsk-ok-state 0) == "ok" then diag-col-good else diag-col-warn) sz (f.gf-len) 0 shift (xdiag-get gfat-cell-write)
    end
  end

 One small write after a refused big one, and whether it landed. The
 2026-08-19 bank ended at the stage before this one, and two readings fit
 it that the bank cannot separate: the rewrite was refused because the
 medium is wedged, or the rewrite was never reached. This answers that
 next time from the glass, which survives when the bank does not.

  dsk-after-name : Integer -> Integer
  dsk-after-name (dummy) =
    let nb = alloc-zeroed 16 16
    in let a = poke-byte nb 0 83
    in let b = poke-byte nb 1 73
    in let c = poke-byte nb 2 78
    in let d = poke-byte nb 3 75
    in let e = poke-byte nb 4 65
    in let f = poke-byte nb 5 32
    in let g = poke-byte nb 6 32
    in let h = poke-byte nb 7 32
    in let i = poke-byte nb 8 67
    in let j = poke-byte nb 9 68
    in let k = poke-byte nb 10 88
    in nb

  dsk-after : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Port] Integer
  dsk-after (vol) =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed 512 512
    in let z = poke-byte buf 0 65
    in let nb = dsk-after-name 0
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      ok <- gfat-write-file vol nb buf 64
      let r = __heap-restore mark
      in if ok then 1 else 0
    end

  dsk-write : Fat16Volume, Integer -> [Device.Port] DiagResult
  dsk-write (vol) (shift) =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed (dsk-size + 512) 512
    in let f = dsk-fill-all buf 0 dsk-size
    in let nb = dsk-name-buf 0
    in let r0 = xdiag-put msc-cell-retry msc-retry-none
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      ok <- gfat-write-file vol nb buf dsk-size
      let r = __heap-restore mark
      in if ok == False then act
        let snap = dsk-snap 0
        in let wstage = xdiag-get gfat-cell-write
        in act
          a <- dsk-after vol
          dsk-finish-in (dsk-write-state snap) diag-col-bad dsk-size 0 0 shift wstage snap a
        end
      end
      else dsk-verify vol shift
    end

Section: The Rung Ladder

 `sink ladder=1` answers a different question from `sink chunk=N`. The chunk
 key returns one bit, and four flights have now returned that bit unchanged
 (`wr=0 cc=256 ph=2 after=0`, the LBA moving only with the allocation). The
 ladder returns the THRESHOLD in one boot: rungs smallest first, stopping at
 the first that gets no completion.

 Smallest first is not a preference. The wedge leaves the endpoint
 unrecovered (`rty=1`, `after=0`), so everything after the first refusal
 fails whatever its size, and a largest-first ladder reads as "everything
 fails".

 Each completed rung writes `SINKL.CDX` BEFORE the next rung is attempted,
 and that ordering is the whole value of the instrument: a wedge at rung N
 still proves rungs 1..N-1 completed only if the record is already on the
 medium when the wedge happens (L-BANK). The driver's own bank is written
 after the stage returns, and the 2026-08-19 sitting watched that bank be
 lost to this very wedge, so the rung record cannot wait for it.

 The payload is 64 KB rather than the 2.7 MB of the single-shot stage: it is
 two transfers at rung 64 and 128 at rung 1, which keeps the smallest rung
 from spending the sitting on time, and it still contains a whole 64-sector
 chunk so rung 64 is a reproduction of the failing case.

 THE PAYLOAD IS FIXED ON PURPOSE AND MUST NOT BE SCALED WITH THE RUNG. The
 rung is the only thing that moves; the total written stays 64 KB at every
 rung. Scaling the payload with the chunk would move two variables at once
 and the threshold this stage reports would no longer mean "the largest
 command the device completes" -- it would confound command size with total
 size, which is the very question the row is trying to separate.

  dsk-rungs : List Integer = [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]
  dsk-rung-size : Integer = 65536

  dsk-ladder-on : DiagCtx -> Boolean
  dsk-ladder-on (c) =
    let v = diag-cfg-value c "sink"
    in if text-starts-with v "ladder=" then text-to-integer (substring v 7 (text-length v - 7)) /= 0 else False

  dsk-ladder-name : Integer -> Integer
  dsk-ladder-name (dummy) =
    let nb = alloc-zeroed 16 16
    in let a = poke-byte nb 0 83
    in let b = poke-byte nb 1 73
    in let c = poke-byte nb 2 78
    in let d = poke-byte nb 3 75
    in let e = poke-byte nb 4 76
    in let f = poke-byte nb 5 32
    in let g = poke-byte nb 6 32
    in let h = poke-byte nb 7 32
    in let i = poke-byte nb 8 67
    in let j = poke-byte nb 9 68
    in let k = poke-byte nb 10 88
    in nb

  dsk-put-text : Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dsk-put-text (buf) (s) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then i
    else let b = poke-byte buf i (to-unicode (char-code-at s i))
    in dsk-put-text buf s (i + 1) n

  dsk-note : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  dsk-note (vol) (done) (rung) =
    let body = "ladder done=" & show done & " rung-sectors=" & show rung & " rung-bytes=" & show (rung * 512) & " payload-bytes=" & show dsk-rung-size & " END"
    in let n = text-length body
    in let buf = alloc-zeroed (n + 512) 512
    in let nb = dsk-ladder-name 0
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in let f = dsk-put-text buf body 0 n
    in act
      ok <- gfat-write-file vol nb buf n
      let r = __heap-restore mark
      in if ok then 1 else 0
    end

  dsk-rung-write : Fat16Volume -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  dsk-rung-write (vol) =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed (dsk-rung-size + 512) 512
    in let f = dsk-fill-all buf 0 dsk-rung-size
    in let nb = dsk-name-buf 0
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in act
      ok <- gfat-write-file vol nb buf dsk-rung-size
      let r = __heap-restore mark
      in ok
    end

  dsk-rung-word : Integer -> Text
  dsk-rung-word (n) = if n == 0 then "none" else show n

  dsk-ladder-lines : Integer, Integer, Integer, DskSnap, Integer -> List Text
  dsk-ladder-lines (done) (rung) (note) (s) (after) =
    ["ladder done=" & show done & " rung-sectors=" & dsk-rung-word rung & " rung-bytes=" & dsk-rung-word (rung * 512) & " payload-bytes=" & show dsk-rung-size & " note=" & show note,
     "wr=" & show (s.ds-wr) & " cc=" & show (s.ds-cc) & " lba=" & show (s.ds-lba) & " rty=" & show (s.ds-rty) & " ph=" & show (s.ds-ph) & " after=" & show after & " chunk=" & show (msc-chunk-now 0)]

  dsk-ladder-stop : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] DiagResult
  dsk-ladder-stop (vol) (done) (rung) (note) =
    let snap = dsk-snap 0
    in act
      a <- dsk-after vol
      let state = if done == 0 then "rung-1-refused" else "ladder-stop"
      in diag-result state diag-col-bad (dsk-ladder-lines done rung note snap a) (dsk-ladder-lines done rung note snap a)
    end

  dsk-ladder-run : Fat16Volume, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] DiagResult
  dsk-ladder-run (vol) (i) (done) (note) =
    if i >= list-length dsk-rungs then
      let snap = dsk-snap 0
      in diag-result "ladder-all" diag-col-good (dsk-ladder-lines done 0 note snap (-1)) (dsk-ladder-lines done 0 note snap (-1))
    else let rung = list-at dsk-rungs i
    in let ch = xdiag-put msc-cell-chunk rung
    in act
      ok <- dsk-rung-write vol
      if ok == False then dsk-ladder-stop vol done rung note
      else act
        n <- dsk-note vol (done + 1) rung
        dsk-ladder-run vol (i + 1) (done + 1) n
      end
    end

Section: Entry

  dsk-run : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port] DiagResult
  dsk-run (c) = act
    if c.dc-bank == False then dsk-finish "no-medium" diag-col-dim dsk-size 0 0 (dsk-shift c) 0
    else let ch = xdiag-put msc-cell-chunk (dsk-msc-chunk c)
    in act
      m <- gfat-mount-esp
      if m.gm-ok == False then dsk-finish "mount-fail" diag-col-bad dsk-size 0 0 (dsk-shift c) (xdiag-get gfat-cell-stage)
      else if dsk-ladder-on c then dsk-ladder-run (m.gm-vol) 0 0 (-1)
      else dsk-write (m.gm-vol) (dsk-shift c)
    end
  end
